id: 1
hanzi: 朝三暮四
pinyin: zhāo sān mù sì
level: HSK 5
part_of_speech: Idiom

meanings_summary:
  en: change one's mind constantly; blow hot and cold; capricious; play fast and loose with tricks
  es: cambiar de opinión constantemente; ser veleta o veleidoso; engañar con triquiñuelas superficiales

senses[2]{sense_number,part_of_speech,register,definition_en,definition_es,example_phrase}:
  1 | Idiom | Standard | (modern sense) to frequently change one's mind, plans, or loyalty; capricious and unreliable | (sentido moderno) cambiar de opinión, planes o fidelidad a cada instante; ser veleidoso o inconstante | 作风朝三暮四 (fickle and erratic style)
  2 | Idiom | Literary | (classical Zhuangzi sense) to deceive others through superficial alterations of form without changing the underlying substance | (sentido clásico) engañar a otros alterando la apariencia sin modificar la realidad sustancial | 弄虚作假 (play deceitful tricks)

example_sentences[2]{id,hanzi,pinyin,en,es}:
  #1 | 职场规划最忌讳的就是朝三暮四，只有锚定长远目标并深耕细作，才能在专业领域有所建树。 | Zhíchǎng guīhuà zuì jìhuì de jiùshì zhāosān-mùsì, zhǐyǒu máodìng chángyuǎn mùbiāo bìng shēngēng-xìzuò, cáinéng zài zhuānyè lǐngyù yǒusuǒ-jiànshù. | What career planning should avoid most is blowing hot and cold; only by anchoring long-term goals and cultivating deeply can one achieve greatness. | Lo que más debe evitarse en la carrera profesional es la veleidad; solo fijando metas a largo plazo y perseverando con paciencia se cosecharán logros señeros.
  #2 | 在《庄子·齐物论》的原初寓言中，老人以朝三暮四戏弄群猴，深刻阐明了事物在本质上并无实质得失的哲理。 | Zài 《Zhuāngzǐ · Qíwùlùn》 de yuánchū yùyán zhōng, lǎorén yǐ zhāosān-mùsì xìnòng qúnhóu, shēnkè chǎnmíng le shìwù zài běnzhì shàng bìng wú shízhì déshī de zhélǐ. | In the original fable from Zhuangzi, the elder tricked monkeys with 'three in morning, four at dusk', elucidating that gains and losses are fundamentally relative. | En la fábula primigenia de Zhuangzi, el anciano burló a los monos cambiando el orden de la comida, revelando que las ganancias y pérdidas son solo apariencias relativas.

