    募 | mù | recruit; raise funds; solicit

semantic_boundary_contrasts:
  core_nuance: 朝三暮四 specifically characterizes inconstancy of will, frequently flipping decisions, or manipulating terms to deceive.
  contrasts[3]{id,compared_word,pinyin,analysis}:
    1 | 朝三暮四 vs. 见异思迁 | jiànyì-sīqiān | 朝三暮四 emphasizes vacillating inconstancy of decisions and whims, whereas 见异思迁 emphasizes abandoning one's current post or partner immediately upon seeing something more attractive.
    2 | 朝三暮四 vs. 朝秦暮楚 | zhāoQín-mùChǔ | 朝三暮四 highlights behavioral or strategic inconsistency, whereas 朝秦暮楚 specifically highlights opportunistic disloyalty in shifting political or personal allegiances.
    3 | 朝三暮四 vs. 出尔反尔 | chū'ěr-fǎn'ěr | 朝三暮四 reflects an internal fickle disposition, while 出尔反尔 refers directly to speaking self-contradictory words and breaking promises.

extended_reading_passages[2]{id,hanzi,pinyin,en,es}:
  #1 | 很多人在浮躁的世风中迷失了自我，今天渴望当作家，明天又向往从商，在朝三暮四的犹豫摇摆中白白虚掷了青春年华。唯有静下心来认清真正的内心热爱，耐得住寂寞深耕细作，才能收获丰硕的生命果实。 | Hěnduō rén zài fúzào de shìfēng zhōng míshī le zìwǒ, jīntiān kěwàng dāng zuòjiā, míngtiān yòu xiàngwǎng cóngshāng, zài zhāosān-mùsì de yóuyù yáobǎi zhōng báibái xūzhì le qīngchūn niánhuá. Wéiyǒu jìngxià xīn lái rènqīng zhēnzhèng de nèixīn rè'ài, nàidezhù jìmò shēngēng-xìzuò, cáinéng shōuhuò fēngshuò de shēngmìng guǒshí. | Many lose themselves in restless social trends, longing to be authors today and entrepreneurs tomorrow, squandering youth in capricious vacillation. Only by quieting the mind to discern genuine passion and enduring quiet labor can life's fruits be harvested. | Muchos se pierden en el torbellino de la frivolidad, deseando hoy ser escritores y mañana negociantes, malgastando sus mejores años en veleidades estériles. Solo serenando el ánimo para abrazar una auténtica vocación y trabajando con constancia se recogen frutos colmados.
  #2 | 回溯中国哲学史，《庄子》中的“朝三暮四”本意在阐明生死齐一、万物玄同的道家智慧，告诫世人切莫被表象的得失所蒙蔽。然而历经千年演变，这一成语在现代语境中已完全转化为对反复无常之举的道德针砭。 | Huísù Zhōngguó zhéxuéshǐ, 《Zhuāngzǐ》 zhōng de 'zhāosān-mùsì' běnyì zài chǎnmíng shēngsǐ-qíyī, wànwù-xuántóng de Dàojiā zhìhuì, gàojiè shìrén qièmò bèi biǎoxiàng de déshī suǒ méngbì. Rán'ér lìjīng qiānnián yǎnbiàn, zhè yì chéngyǔ zài xiàndài yǔjìng zhōng yǐ wánquán zhuǎnhuà wéi duì fǎnfù-wúcháng zhī jǔ de dàodé zhēnbiān. | Tracing Chinese philosophy, Zhuangzi's 'Zhao San Mu Si' originally elucidated Daoist wisdom on the equality of things, warning against illusion. Through millennia of evolution, this idiom has completely transformed into a moral rebuke of capricious fickle behavior. | En la filosofía china, la fábula de Zhuangzi ilustraba la relatividad de ganancias y pérdidas exhortando a no engañarse por las apariencias. Sin embargo, tras siglos de evolución lingüística, el modismo censura hoy la inconstancia de ánimo y la falta de palabra.
